<section class="service-page-intro">
  <div><p class="eyebrow">Digital resources</p><h2>Use interaction where it gives the audience something useful to do.</h2></div>
  <div class="prose"><p>A digital format can let someone move through material at their own pace, compare different views or reach the level of detail relevant to them. It can also make completed research and programme outputs easier to share beyond their original audience.</p><p>We begin with the content and user need, then select the lightest format that will do the job well. That keeps the experience focused, responsive and maintainable rather than adding interaction for its own sake.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="section-shell service-offers">
  <div class="split-heading"><h2>Useful digital forms for complex communication<span class="red-stop">.</span></h2><p>The output is shaped around how people need to find, understand and use the material.</p></div>
  <div class="service-offer-grid">
    <article><span>01</span><h3>Interactive explainers</h3><p>Guided experiences that reveal stages, comparisons or levels of detail without overwhelming the first view.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h3>Research microsites</h3><p>Focused sites that give programmes, findings and supporting resources a clear and accessible digital home.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h3>Animated communication</h3><p>Motion used to explain sequence, change and mechanism across presentations, films or digital resources.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h3>Digital documents</h3><p>Responsive or interactive alternatives to fixed publications when readers need navigation and flexibility.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

